<refentry id="gmime-streams">
  <refmeta>
    <refentrytitle>Streams</refentrytitle>
    <refmiscinfo>GMime Library</refmiscinfo>
  </refmeta>

  <refnamediv>
    <refname>Streams</refname>
    <refpurpose>How to use Streams</refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>
  <refsect1 id="stream-overview">
    <title>Overview of Streams</title>
    <para>Streams are the fundamental method for reading and writing
    data used by GMime. You'll probably notice that the basic API is 
    similar to that of the low-level Unix I/O layer (read(), write(), 
    lseek(), etc) with some additional niceties such as a printf-like 
    function.</para>

    <para>The three (3) basic stream types are: GMimeStreamFile,
    GMimeStreamFs and GMimeStreamMem. You can manipulate all three
    streams using the GMimeStream interfaces. In addition, some of
    these streams have extended interfaces to allow more fine grained
    manipulation.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamFile and GMimeStreamFs are very similar in that
    they are both meant for reading and writing data to the file
    system (in the form of files). Since GMimeStreamFile is an
    abstracted layer above the standard libc FILE type, one of the
    added benefits is buffered I/O. GMimeStreamFs, on the other hand,
    is an abstracted layer above Unix file descriptors. While a
    GMimeStreamFs can be used on top of a UNIX socket or pipe, you
    must be careful because sockets and pipes are not seekable
    (see GMimeStreamPipe for a solution to this).</para>

    <para>Unlike the previous 2 stream types, GMimeStreamMem does not
    interact with the file system at all (except maybe the swap
    partition/file indirectly). Memory streams are handy when you want
    reads and writes to be nearly instantaneous and/or if you don't
    want to create a temporary file on disk.</para>

    <para>Use g_mime_stream_substream() for bounded views rather than a
    separate bounded-stream class. Start and end use absolute backend
    coordinates, and ranges must fit inside their parent's bounds.
    An end of -1 inherits a finite parent's end; empty ranges are valid.
    Invalid ranges fail with EINVAL before backend dispatch. Each backend
    still defines its own seek and explicit-close behavior.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamMem substreams retain their shared byte array, so
    explicitly closing a parent or replacing its buffer does not invalidate
    existing views. Positions and bounds are independent. Reads stop at the
    actual allocation even if the declared finite range extends farther;
    the finite length still reports that range. Writes can grow storage and
    zero newly exposed gaps, stopping with a partial count or zero at a full
    end bound. SEEK_SET is absolute, and finite SEEK_END uses the view's
    end. Unbounded seeks can grow storage, but seeking to the current
    position does not regrow externally truncated data. Invalid origins
    fail with EINVAL; overflowing offsets, write counts or storage sizes
    fail with EOVERFLOW.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamFs views also retain independent logical positions and
    EOF state, but borrow the same descriptor. Reads reposition it for the
    current view and return zero at a finite end. Cached EOF does not
    suppress subsequent reads after another writer extends the file.
    Unbounded length queries use the actual file extent, reporting zero
    when it ends before the view's start. Writes retain partial counts and
    fail with EINVAL at a full finite bound. Seeks do not extend the file;
    positive SEEK_END offsets use physical EOF, zero and negative finite
    offsets use the declared end, and unbounded cached EOF supplies the
    observed end for nonpositive offsets. Invalid origins fail with EINVAL;
    overflowing counts, arithmetic or off_t conversions fail with EOVERFLOW.
    Windows I/O counts are additionally limited to G_MAXINT for the CRT API.
    Finish using all views before explicitly closing an owning parent:
    object retention alone does not keep its descriptor open.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamBlock stores memory content in separate 2048-byte blocks,
    avoiding repeated copies of existing bytes while growing. Unlike
    GMimeStreamMem, it does not allow seeking past the stored extent; extend it
    first with g_mime_stream_block_set_length(). Growth exposes zeros, and
    shrinking clears the discarded tail. Use
    g_mime_stream_block_to_byte_array() for an independent contiguous copy
    without moving the position. Native substreams share storage and length,
    but retain independent positions and bounds. Closing one view does not
    invalidate the others. Reset only rewinds. Native bounds and SEEK_SET
    positions are absolute.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamCat concatenates child streams in insertion order.
    Reads fill the requested buffer across child boundaries, starting at
    each child's current position without seeking or resetting it. This
    permits reading nonseekable sources; explicitly seeking or resetting
    the concatenation still requires repositionable children. Empty or
    exhausted concatenations return zero, and adding a source after EOF
    resumes reading. A transient zero read without a child EOF indication
    does not skip that child. On an error after partial progress, the bytes
    already read are returned and the failing child remains current for
    the next read. Sources are retained by reference and are not explicitly
    closed when the concatenation releases them.</para>

    <para>Concatenated writes also use each child's current position.
    Earlier children retain their existing lengths and must support length
    and position queries; the last child may grow and can be a pipe or
    filtered destination. A full child is flushed before spilling into the
    next child, but not when a write merely fills it exactly. Short writes
    are retried on the same child. Write or boundary-flush errors preserve
    accepted bytes and leave the failing child current for retry. Explicit
    flush affects only the current child and does nothing when there is no
    current child; writing after read-side EOF resumes at the last child.
    Read EOF stays cached across writes until a child is added or the
    concatenation is reset or repositioned, without losing the current flush
    destination. Native child and concatenation bounds still apply, and
    zero-progress writes fail
    with EIO rather than skipping destinations.</para>

    <para>Forward concatenation seeks physically position traversed children
    and leave a child selected when the target lands exactly at its end.
    This permits seeking to the chain's end while retaining the final flush
    destination. Backward seeks reposition only the selected child and the
    children up to the previous current child; they seek rather than reset
    those children. Seeking to the current position leaves cached EOF
    unchanged. Child seek errors preserve already completed positioning;
    an explicit reset rewinds all children even at the logical beginning,
    recovering their positions after a partial backward seek.
    Native bounds still apply, and overflowing length sums or seek offsets
    fail with EOVERFLOW.</para>

    <para>The five (5) advanced stream types are GMimeStreamMmap,
    GMimeStreamNull, GMimeStreamPipe, GMimeStreamBuffer and
    GMimeStreamFilter.</para>

    <para>Our most simple stream, GMimeStreamNull, is the stream
    equivalent of /dev/null on Unix systems. The main difference is
    that GMimeStreamNull records the number of bytes written to it.
    Its logical length tracks the highest written position, so overwrites
    do not inflate the measured size. The written and optional newlines
    counters remain cumulative, including overwrites. Use
    g_mime_stream_null_set_length() to resize the measurement without
    recording output. Seeks outside the measured extent fail rather than
    clamping, and nonempty reads fail with EBADF. Native bounds limit writes;
    setting length updates a finite end bound relative to its start.
    Reset clears measurement and statistics while retaining bounds and the
    newline-counting option. This is useful for measuring how many bytes a
    GMimeEntity (for example) will require without storing its output.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamMmap is a memory-mapped stream. This isn't
    guaranteed to work on all systems since not all systems support
    the POSIX mmap system call, but for those that do - this might
    present a faster stream than GMimeStreamFs and/or
    GMimeStreamFile. You'll have to do some experimentation to know
    for sure.</para>

    <para>GMimeStreamPipe is just like GMimeStreamFs except that it
    doesn't support seeking, thus being perfect for use with sockets
    and pipes.</para>

    <para>The GMimeStreamBuffer can be used on top of any other type
    of stream and has 3 modes: block reads, block writes, and cached
    reads. Block reads are especially useful if you will be making a
    lot of small reads from a stream that accesses the file
    system. Block writes are useful for very much the same reason. The
    final mode, cached reads, can become memory intensive but can be
    very helpful when inheriting from a stream that does not support
    seeking (Note: this mode is the least tested so be careful using
    it).</para>

    <para>Our final stream type, GMimeStreamFilter, can also be used
    on top of another stream. This stream, as you may have guessed,
    filters reads and writes to its inherited stream. For example, one
    could write a compression filter and apply it to a
    GMimeStreamFilter and any further reads or writes would be
    (un)compressed.</para>

    <para>Filter membership is checked by instance identity. Removing
    a filter instance removes its first occurrence; the existing ID-based
    removal identifies a particular occurrence. Duplicate entries each
    retain a reference. The source accessor returns a borrowed stream.
    Avoid changing the chain or manipulating the source while filtered
    output is buffered.</para>

    <para>Flushing GMimeStreamFilter completes its write-side filters
    once until more data is written. Failed completion writes can be
    retried with flush or close without duplicating accepted output.
    Close reports completion failures before closing an owned source.
    Read-side completion occurs once at source EOF, and its output is
    drained before EOF is reported. Reset discards buffered output and
    resets both the source and the filters. Flush does not flush the
    underlying source itself; flush that stream separately when needed.
    Explicitly flush or close to finish writes before releasing the
    stream.</para>
  </refsect1>
</refentry>
